well every three years the council has  to produce a long-term plan so for the  2015  2025 year the council has to spell out  its program of works and its levels of  service and how it's going to fund those  so we've got some major issues in the  next 10 years that we have to to get to  grips with and we're asking the  ratepayers and the residents to give us  some feedback on those  key issues now some of those key issues  include reducing the levels of service  for example the  government or the nzta are reducing its  funding for roads uh by one percent a  year so they're roughly giving us sixty  percent at the moment that's going to  reduce by one percent so we we are  suggesting that the level of of service  for roads will will be less smooth and  they'll reduce over that 10-year period  and our footpath program is going to  reduce as well so we need some feedback  on that there are some works that we  have to do and we've got a problem with  sewage treatment for kennington  residents so they're going to connect on  to the pipeline and that's going to come  into the the city  surge treatment plant the wastewater  treatment plant at clifton needs to have  some money spent about a million dollars  to upgrade it so that the consents can  still be  used going forward in the future but the  major issue that's facing us  infrastructure wise is our water pipes  we've got asbestos pipes about 40  percent  of our network is water pipes and some  testing has shown that those pipes may  not last the 100 years or the 65 years  that they were expected to do  and so therefore we're going to have to  progressively increase our program of  water improvement and replacing those  pipes so that's going to cost over 20  million in the next 10 year  plan so we need some feedback we've also  looking at the levels of service for  parks and reserves and that's you know  we're looking at we've got a very high  level of parks compared to the national  average and some of those parks aren't  getting utilized so what we've decided  to do is maybe sell some of those off  and reduce those those number of parks  and transfer the money into the existing  parks that are getting well used that  need new new equipment we're also  looking at the splash palace which is  really a success story and it's getting  a lot of use at the moment at splash  palace  but the changing facilities there are  inadequate and we're also looking at a  fitness centre there as well to help  cope with help pay for that  we need to extend splash palace really  for two main reasons there are two key  components i think to this first of all  we're exceeding by a long way the the  design limitations on the building and  and i mean that in terms of the number  of people that we have entering the  facility so last year for example we had  over 370 000 people come through and and  the numbers were just never estimated to  be that high and as a result of that  we're running out of things like  changing room space  car parking is an issue  we see problems in our cafe and foyer  area um  getting comprised because of the number  of people in there so these plans really  address that issue the second thing of  course is that we've had feedback over  the years that suggest people would like  to see a gymnasium at splash palace  and and what we believe we can do  through the addition of a gym is that we  can fund both the gymnasium and the  additional change room space and  four-year space  and achieve kill two birds with one  stone if you like it's funded through  loan funding so we would take a loan for  the entire works and that loan would be  repaid through user charges over over 20  years so there's no rate funding that  goes into this project  councils produced a park strategy which  looked at the appropriateness of ground  and reserve areas in the empirical  district and each of those areas have a  different category whether it's a  playground or a sports field or whatever  and that has revealed some surpluses in  some areas and also then they've  produced a play strategy which indicated  where the play equipment should be  relating to where the children are in  their makoko district currently we have  80 neighborhood  parks  and each of those have got one or two  pieces of equipment on this will be  reduced down to 52 which will have a  better range of equipment on  for children to play give them greater  play experience  at this stage they're considering  disposal of approximately 10 areas  and a lot of these undeveloped areas  areas that have been obtained in the  past where the city hasn't grown  and  so the idea is to rationalize those and  end up with  reserve areas um they'll go through this  process people can consult through the  long-term plan on whether they want to  see the reserve disappear from the area  and if council decides to go ahead with  it we'll then advertise again via the  reserves act and give people a second  chance at putting submissions in on it  at the moment in the consultation  document there's three prime areas this  firstly there's the water supply issue  where we're looking to renew renew the  ic mains earlier than we had expected  and within the stormwater and surge  areas there's our pipes that are coming  to the end of their lives as well and  they need to be they need to be replaced  and in the roading area there are some  changes to the way it's funded and the  level of service that we're looking to  provide going forward within the water  pipes the the ac pipes were laid  asbestos cement pipes were laid in the  1950s through the 1980s and they had an  original life of about 65 years but what  we've found through some recent bursts  and some sampling is that those pipes  are starting to fail earlier than we had  expected so within the consultation  document there is a proposal to actually  bring those planned renewals  forward from where they previously were  so that's about 55 million over the next  10 years as planned to upgrade those  pipes  so what that will mean is that there is  a rate there is a rise in the amount  that ratepayers are asked to contribute  for the water for the water section and  within the consultation document it  gives some options that they can select  and give some feedback on  within roading there's there's two  things that are happening one is the new  zealand transport agency provide council  and other councils throughout the  country with  funding and what's been indicated over  the next nine years that's going to  reduce by one percent so what we're  finding what we will find is that  ratepayers will have to contribute more  but at the same time they've also  indicated that our roads are actually  smoother than the national average of  roads so what they're also asking us is  for us to do less less work on them and  then they reduce to their towards that  average and that's over the next nine  years that will be occurring  on an individual carriageway section by  section people won't see much but what  it does mean is that they will get  slightly rougher and we'll have to focus  and prioritise where we actually  undertake works  the real problem that we're looking at  and asking for feedback on is that the  rate increase for next year is proposed  to be 3.71 and an average of about 3.8  over the next 10 years we've got a lot  of people on fixed incomes and  affordability issues are a concern  so we want to get get some feedback from  the ratepayers and residents how they  feel about it are we spending too much  are we not spending enough on the right  areas are the levels of service that  we're proposing to provide the next 10  years are they adequate or not adequate  so submissions starting on the 25th  of april and go through to the 25th of  may you can get a copy of our website or  from the service center in bluff the  library or at the civic administration  building  and  submissions are open and we'd like to  get your feedback and  input into those  you

